e  can have the baby turkey swallow it and  what you can see  from these blue images is in the green  is where the viral protein or the cub  protein has gone in the south and then  what we do is it's it's very high-tech  we watch the turkeys go to the bathroom  and we came up with the rating score for  diarrhea and you can see there's one two  three four where we would consider for a  diarrhea and this is actually based on  what we see in humans so Google Bristol  stool chart and you can see that in  humans are seven different types and  it'll tell you what it will look like  the consistency and also what it tells  you about the health so using this  rating and knowing that this this viral  protein could get to the intestines we  asked what happens do the turkeys get  diarrhea and the answer is yes if you  look at the percent of birds with a  clinical score greater than three we can  see that this coat protein alone induces  them to have diarrhea in a concentration  dependent manner so we need very little  protein to have this happen so that  would suggest that this astro virus  capsid is a toxin how is it working well  as I told you what we saw in cell  culture is that it causes these  membranes to open up and breaks down  this barrier and we see exactly the same  thing in these baby turkeys so the top  bar graph is actually the epithelial  with the ions running through it to show  you the barrier and the bottom is  looking at those fluorescent dyes going  through the membrane so here's what  we're thinking you have this virus  getting to your intestines and most of  the time what it does is causes damage  to those cells and the cells die and you  can see from these purple images the  ones on the left are healthy intestines  the ones on the right are damaged we do  not have again anything like that  happening in the astra virus you  in animals in humans or in cell cultures  so what could be happening so Astro  virus is not killing the sound so how is  it breaking down this barrier well if  you look at how these cells are held  together in the intestines is what you  have is a cell one on the left the cell  2 on the right and they're held together  through a series of protein protein  interactions that make these cells  actually fit together and the top parts  are called tight junctions the next or  middle level are called adherence  junctions and then the bottom are called  desmosomes these are absolutely crucial  for forming this barrier and this is not  specific to an intestinal epithelial  cells you see this in all of your  epithelial cells as well as your  endothelial cells so what we think is  that the virus is actually unzipping or  pulling apart these cells so how is it  doing that and how do we know that so  here are images and that left-most are  uninfected cells and what we're looking  at is microscopy staining for these  individual cell cell proteins and you  were looking down on them as a  cross-section and you see this nice  cobblestone morphology and that is  actually the periphery of the cells  being held together in the top being  occluded 0 1 beta catenin and ecad hiren  so going from the top of the cell to the  bottom and what you can see is at  different times post infection these  cells actually are these proteins  actually come apart or move away from  the membrane and we lose them and this  is very evident at 24 hours if you  compare your MOC to 24 hours post  infection what we see is we've lost the  cell-cell contacts so basically that  virus is not killing the cell but it's  unzipping them and they're coming apart  like  but it's actually more complicated and  interesting than that it's completely  changing the sounds so these are  epithelial cells they are very important  for maintaining this barrier they have a  top in the bottom and what the astra  virus is doing is causing them to change  and actually become more migratory they  completely change shape these are the  kinds of changes in cells we see when  cancer happens and a cell goes from  being normal to cancerous it goes  through this epithelial to mesenchymal  transition and Astro virus is causing  the same sort of cellular change not  causing cancer just causing the cells to  change shape and how do we know that so  again the way that we can test that is  by first looking at microscopy and the  top panel is an epithelial protein eat  cat urine we're again you can see it's  at very high levels in MOC and it's gone  by 24 hours post infection in contrast  if we look for a protein that's only  found on these mesenchymal or more  migratory cells you can see in  uninfected cells there's nothing there  but after you've infected you can see  this increase in this protein in the  infected cells the other thing that  happens is that epithelial cells again  they have a top and a bottom meaning  they're polarized and this is again  important for their function and Astro  virus we lose that polarity so if you  look at the middle or the mock panel  what you can see are three different  colors red is a protein at the top of  the cell blue is the nucleus and green  is a protein that stains at the bottom  and sides of the South very clear  polarity now with an Astro virus  infection which is the next panel down  what you can see is that these layers  are messed up that's a very scientific  word I know but they are damaged you  no longer have the top at the top you  don't have things at the bottom so we  have changed the polarity of the sound  that's part of what makes it able to  start moving do we see this in vivo and  indeed we do we see the same thing when  we look in those baby turkeys so if you  look in their intestines for a protein  that should only be on the outside and  that would be in the one PBS and you can  see the green staining when we've added  that astro protein what you can see is  all that green staining protein has now  moved to the inside of the cell so we've  lost polarity in the intestine now your  intestine has got to maintain this  barrier it has to maintain polarity  because that's very important for  controlling fluids for controlling how  the gut responds and if any of this has  been damaged or is not working properly  you get what's considered leaky gut you  do not want leaky gut syndrome syndrome  there are a number of clinical  conditions associated with leaky gut or  increased intestinal permeability like  IBS Crohn's disease intestinal infection  but even things like alcoholism eczema  there's a number of syndromes and  diseases associated with having a leaky  gut so we have after virus going in the  coat protein causing the membrane is to  increase but yet we don't have leaky gut  syndrome why is that the reason is if  the virus has evolved a very clever way  of getting into the the cells it needs  growing and continuing to spread because  that's the goal of any viruses to get in  to replicate to make more virus come out  and infect the next person and cell and  what we found is that the Astra virus  actually is infecting goblet cells and  you can see that in the very first image  the red very red staining are infected  goblet cells and these are actually the  main producers of mucus and mucus is  absolutely crucial for gut health you  have to have mucus there it protects you  from pathogens it helps you maintain  your microbiome it helps you repair if  there's damage the astra virus is going  in and replicating in themselves and  this is very unique very few cells or  viruses have been shown to replicate in  a goblet cell they are only four to ten  percent of the population cells in your  entire gut very very low levels and what  we found is by actually being able to  isolate those very few cells and look at  what genes are being regulated well  here's a picture sorry this is a goblet  cell as you can see and here is a straw  virus within this goblet cells very  striking microscopy pictures so we got  these goblet cells and we're able to  isolate them and we asked what are the  genes look like so what is the virus  doing to those cells what we found it's  doing is actually increasing mucus  production any types of protein  associating with secreting that mucus as  well as a very protective molecule  called ido1 it is up regulating all of  these and we know they are absolutely  crucial in order to help the virus  replicate but also protect the host from  damage from the virus so to conclude we  have this very common enteric virus  leading cause of diarrhea especially  kids under the  to immunocompromised people and agent  adults but they're under diagnosed and  we don't understand much about disease  we do know that disease can range from  no symptoms at all to mild to severe  wasting diarrhea to systemic even fatal  brain infections but it doesn't appear  to work like other enteric viruses most  other viruses will go in they will  damage the cells in your intestines and  then your intestine won't work properly  in this case Astro virus outer coat  protein alone acts like a toxin it binds  to something on the cells that causes  cells to change lose their shape and  normal functions which could be bad but  when the virus gets in and begins  replicating it does control the cells  it's infecting to increase mucus  production and basically protect your  body and the host from getting leaky gut  syndrome so we're now asking either a  role for Astro virus and other gut  diseases and more importantly how do we  treat these if they can cause systemic  and even wasting especially in our  high-risk populations how are we gonna  treat this and treatment has  historically been oral rehydration  therapy but very recently we have found  an antiviral compound that will work  even after you're infected with Astro  virus which is very exciting and that is  a conclusion of my talk and of course I  have to thank everybody in my laboratory  because they do all the work everybody  at st. Jude the faculty people that help  us in the veterinary pathology Corps our  collaborators and our funding sources  and I thank you for participating and I  encourage you to ask questions and we  will respond by email share our passion  for both diarrhea and Astro viruses  thank you
 

